> On Thursday, January 15, 2015 08:12:24 guskant wrote:
> > Just for your info. Considering application of Ci/uV -> Cii/uuV to
> fu'ivla,
> > not only those beginning with CgV but also those in the form
> (C)V(g)CCgV...
> > produce tosmabru-like problem. I picked up such fu'ivla from an xml dump
> > dated 2015-01-08:
> >
> > eskua, februa, fiakre, fiesta, kuadranta, kuaidzi, madjio, matriocka,
> > patriarko, reskuamata, restuatara, tiatro, tuitsku
>
> "reskuamata" and "restuatara" appear to be badly, and differently, formed
> from
> "Squamata" and "tuatara" with the gism "res".
>
> Changing Cu/iV to Cuu/iiV or Cu/i'V at the end of a word puts the accent on
> what was a glide; changing it to Cuu/iiV at the beginning, if there's a
> single
> consonant at the beginning, results in no consonant pair in the first five
> letters.
>
> On Thursday, January 15, 2015 21:31:44 Gleki Arxokuna wrote:
> > eskua => sku'ate
> > For Gregorian months something like {jangre'i} for Jan., {febgre'i} for
> > Feb. etc. (or whatever suffix you invent). We can easily borrow latin
> names
> > anyway ({madjio} is the worst Lojbanization of a latin name i've ever
> > seen).
>
> "madjio" is from Italian.


And that's what I don't like. I suppose names should follow some system,
not be arbitrarily taken from different languages within one set of words
(then why not Lojbanize Italian "gennaio" and so on?).

Here is one more attempt (same problems)
janmese febmese marmese primese majmese jonmese julmese augmese sepmese
oktomese novmese dekmese (June is {jon-} to make it more distinguishable
from {jul-}.)

For Italian system
gennaio febbraio marzo aprile  maggio giugno luglio agosto settembre ottobre
novembre dicembre

let me try:
jenmese febmese marmese primese majmese junmese lulmese agmese, setmese,
otmese, novmese dicmese


This might be only easier to remember since only the first letters of
months are retained + a pseudo-suffix is added.




> This change affects half the month names, including
> all the ones that couldn't be entered while jbovlaste was using vlatai:
> ianvari
> frebuari -> frebuuari
> martio ->?
> prilio ->?
> madjio ->?
> djunio ->?
> djulio ->?
> avgusto
> septembero
> oktobero
> novmbero
> decmbero
> and the obsolete names and leap month:
> kuintili -> ku'intili
> sektili
> mercedonio ->?
>
> Pierre
